19c97ab03536b32de36f2dc637b8a","kind":"ARTIFACT","path":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v3/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-088c64de4afcdfcbe118380c3a4552e1f5f48c619858ff1f690a347dfb6d4788.json","producedBy":{"role":"REVIEWER","threadId":null,"tool":"shadow-council-harness"},"sourcePath":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v3/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-088c64de4afcdfcbe118380c3a4552e1f5f48c619858ff1f690a347dfb6d4788.json","summary":"The candidate contradicts the required zero execution-effect consumers: it explicitly states that downstream execution tiers consume V1C roots and receipts, creating execution-consumer dependencies despite declaring none. Its asynchronous pull model also cannot guarantee that a stopEpoch increment “instantaneously” invalidates grants across downstream checks; stale, partitioned, or compromised consumers may continue acting without a freshness protocol or fail-closed rule. The stop register lacks a defined linearizable authority, persistence/rollback protection, and transaction ordering relative to ledger commits. Privilege separation is further weakened by combining ledger verification, R3 grant administration, import validation, and emergency-stop authority inside one broadly described enclave, while key roles, interfaces, quorum rules, and failure modes remain unspecified. Thus the proposal is neither internally consistent nor exact enough to establish the requested guarantees."}],"evidenceManifestHash":"sha256:f090b7c0dfbbf3a53376b447dba52a720aec1da592d98b00d51d83fbece6476e"}
